Spec Kit assets bundled with Maestro
====================================

Source:   https://github.com/github/spec-kit
Version:  v1.2.3 (tag), commit 8ac9e8103319d98099cfc274a2517bb2b621626c, released 2026-10-22
License:  MIT License, Copyright GitHub, Inc.
          The full license text is reproduced in THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md at the root of the
          Maestro repository and inside the Maestro package.

Files copied verbatim from the tag above:
  templates/spec-template.md, templates/plan-template.md, templates/tasks-template.md,
  templates/checklist-template.md, templates/constitution-template.md,
  templates/commands/{analyze,checklist,constitution,converge,implement,tasks,taskstoissues}.md,
  scripts/bash/*.sh, scripts/powershell/*.ps1, scripts/python/*.py

Files copied from spec-kit 0.4.5.dev0 (the agent-file template left the core in 1.x):
  templates/agent-file-template.md

Files modified for Maestro (the original text is at the tag above):
  templates/commands/specify.md
    - frontmatter: added `scripts` (create-new-feature) so Maestro resolves {SCRIPT}
    - steps 2–3: the feature directory, spec file, feature.json and git branch are created by the
      native create-new-feature step instead of a hook plus manual directory creation
    - clarification questions: note to ask them through Maestro's ask_user tool
  templates/commands/plan.md
    - Phase 1: added step 4 "Agent context update" (native update-agent-context step,
      MAESTRO.md plus existing agent files)
  templates/commands/clarify.md
    - question loop: note to ask each question through Maestro's ask_user tool

Maestro never executes the bundled scripts; they are installed into projects by /speckit.init so
that other coding agents keep working on the same repository. The native steps in Maestro
(src/speckit/) reproduce their behaviour.
